<Project>
  <PropertyGroup>
    <!-- Source capability version. Release packaging passes -p:Version, which is
         visible here and wins. A plain source build, including the main CI build, does
         not, and the SDK's later 1.0.0-0.local package identity must not become this
         stamp.
         Provider admission compares it with MinEngineVersion, so the fallback has to
         stay at or above the newest version heading in CHANGELOG.md (the release being
         prepared, or the latest published one). A stale fallback refuses providers the
         source can run. Hosts that build the step catalogue with
         StepKindRegistry.BuildAndFreeze without the admission callback, such as the
         ledger sample's custom runner, never consult this stamp, so it does not affect
         them. -->
    <VouchfxEngineVersion Condition="'$(Version)' != ''">$(Version)</VouchfxEngineVersion>
    <VouchfxEngineVersion Condition="'$(VouchfxEngineVersion)' == ''">1.3.0</VouchfxEngineVersion>
    <TargetFramework>net8.0</TargetFramework>
    <!-- Retain the .NET 8 API baseline while allowing the CLI, test hosts and
         executable fixtures to run when only a newer major runtime is installed. -->
    <RollForward>Major</RollForward>
    <LangVersion>11</LangVersion>
    <Nullable>enable</Nullable>
    <ImplicitUsings>enable</ImplicitUsings>
    <TreatWarningsAsErrors>true</TreatWarningsAsErrors>
    <EnforceCodeStyleInBuild>true</EnforceCodeStyleInBuild>
    <AnalysisLevel>latest-recommended</AnalysisLevel>
    <Deterministic>true</Deterministic>
    <!-- Not packable by default: only projects that opt in (the Provider SDK,
         which sets <IsPackable>true</IsPackable>) produce a NuGet package. This
         keeps `dotnet pack vouchfx.sln` from emitting engine/provider/test
         packages (S08 T4 / S08-F-03). Test projects also pin this to false in
         tests/Directory.Build.props. -->
    <IsPackable>false</IsPackable>
    <!-- Doc-comment gate (#430, #490). The C# compiler only PARSES XML doc comments
         when it is asked to emit a documentation file; without this property it
         silently ignores them, so malformed and wrong doc XML shipped invisibly —
         `dotnet build -warnaserror` never saw it, `dotnet format` does not validate
         doc XML, and the repo's Roslyn censuses parse with `descendIntoTrivia: false`,
         which makes comments invisible by construction. Before this line only 6 of
         77 projects opted in individually, so the blind spot covered the engine
         (including Vouchfx.Engine.Orchestration, the largest and most heavily
         documented project) and every test assembly. Setting it HERE — once, for
         every project — is the whole point: a partial rollout recreates the blind
         spot in whichever projects are missed.

         Combined with TreatWarningsAsErrors above, a malformed or unresolvable doc
         comment is now a BUILD ERROR: CS1570 (malformed XML), CS1571 (duplicate
         <param>), CS1572 (<param> for a non-existent parameter), CS1574/CS1580/CS1581
         (unresolvable cref), CS1587 (doc comment not on a valid language element),
         CS1734 (<paramref> for a non-existent parameter) and CS0419 (cref matching
         several overloads — add the signature, e.g. `M(int)` rather than `M`).

         SUPPRESSED BY DEFAULT, AND ONLY BY DEFAULT — do NOT "helpfully" delete the
         opt-out that goes with them:
           CS1591  missing XML comment for a publicly visible type or member
           CS1573  parameter has no matching <param> tag
         This repository does not document every public member and does not intend
         to; requiring that across 77 projects is a different and far larger project
         than the gate this property exists to provide. What is wanted is only
         *malformed* or *wrong* documentation — a comment that names the wrong thing
         is a defect; an absent comment is a choice. Suppressing these two keeps the
         gate mechanical and false-positive free, which is what makes it survivable.

         BUT SIX PROJECTS ALREADY ENFORCED MORE THAN THAT, and a blanket NoWarn here
         would silently take it away from them. Vouchfx.Sdk, Vouchfx.Sdk.Testing,
         Vouchfx.Engine.Abstractions, .Authoring, .Compilation and .Planning each set
         GenerateDocumentationFile=true under the TreatWarningsAsErrors above with NO
         NoWarn of their own, which made CS1591 and CS1573 build ERRORS in those
         projects — two of them (Vouchfx.Sdk, Vouchfx.Sdk.Testing) being SHIPPED
         packages whose XML docs are part of the package contract. Relaxing that as a
         side effect of a repo-wide default would be a gate loss, invisible until the
         first undocumented public member appeared in a published API. So the
         suppression is applied in Directory.Build.targets instead, conditioned on
         VouchfxDocumentEveryPublicMember, and each of those six sets that property to
         true. Nothing regresses today (measured: none of the six emits either warning
         at the time of writing) — this keeps it that way.

         ONE PUBLISHED PACKAGE WOULD HAVE GROWN, and it is not one of the six.
         Vouchfx.Cli is IsPackable + PackAsTool, and PackAsTool packs the PUBLISH output,
         so a documentation file for every reachable assembly — the engine and all 25 Core
         providers — would have been shipped inside tools/net8.0/any. That is megabytes of
         internal engineering prose in a package nothing compiles against, and it carries
         the sprint/task identifiers CLAUDE.md's publication boundary keeps off every
         published surface. It does NOT ship: Vouchfx.Cli.csproj sets
         PublishDocumentationFiles=false, which drops the XML from publish while leaving
         THIS property — and therefore the gate — untouched. The library packages
         (Vouchfx.Sdk, Vouchfx.Sdk.Testing) keep theirs, because for a library the XML is
         part of the package contract. Do not "tidy" that opt-out away. -->
    <GenerateDocumentationFile>true</GenerateDocumentationFile>

    <!-- #550: build.yml's unit-test step starts one test host per project (`dotnet test
         vouchfx.sln`), and each host's trx name must carry the PROJECT, not just a prefix
         and a second-resolution timestamp, so two hosts finishing in the same second
         cannot silently drop one host's results. See that workflow's own comment on the
         `Unit tests` step for the measured collision mode this replaces and the run that
         confirmed the gap was still theoretical, not yet triggered.

         Set HERE, once, rather than as a `dotnet test` logger argument on the command line
         in the workflow: `dotnet test <solution>` forwards a CLI logger argument to every
         project as an MSBuild GLOBAL property (effectively `-p:VSTestLogger=…`), and a
         global property set on the command line always wins over a PropertyGroup value a
         project — or anything it imports — sets, so a CLI flag would apply the identical
         literal prefix to every host again and undo the whole point. A PropertyGroup value
         set here is exactly what a CLI flag cannot express: one that expands
         $(MSBuildProjectName) DIFFERENTLY per project.

         Set at the REPO ROOT, not only in tests/Directory.Build.props, for the same
         reason GenerateDocumentationFile is set here rather than per project above: it is
         what reaches examples/*.Tests as well as tests/*.Tests with no second copy to
         fall out of step — examples/Directory.Build.props already imports this file. It
         is inert on every non-test project (the VSTest target that reads it never runs
         there), so setting it once for the whole tree costs nothing.

         %3b is the MSBuild-property-safe encoding of the literal `;` the trx logger's own
         `trx;Param=value` argument syntax needs: an unescaped `;` here would instead be
         read as a second, separate list entry by the MSBuild item evaluation the SDK's
         test targets pass this property through, splitting "trx" and "LogFilePrefix=…"
         apart. LogFilePrefix, not LogFileName, keeps the per-run timestamp the original
         #529 choice measured as load-bearing (see build.yml) — this only adds the project
         component that timestamp never carried. -->
    <VSTestLogger>trx%3bLogFilePrefix=unit_$(MSBuildProjectName)</VSTestLogger>
  </PropertyGroup>

  <ItemGroup>
    <!-- NuGet audit suppression for ONE advisory (ZEB-33): GHSA-f7vh-h5g3-7jrh,
         high severity, a macOS Aspire dashboard cookie leak via a YARP link. It affects
         Aspire.Hosting and Aspire.Hosting.AppHost below 13.6.5, and 13.6.5 is the only patched
         release; the pin is 13.4.2 (Directory.Packages.props). Without this item NuGet audit
         reports NU1903 for both packages, which TreatWarningsAsErrors above turns into a restore
         failure on every build. NuGetAuditLevel, NuGetAuditMode, NoWarn and
         WarningsNotAsErrors are deliberately untouched, so every other advisory NuGet audit
         reports under the current settings still fails restore. This entry must stay in this
         file only: the guard test fails CI if an audit suppression or audit-level
         override appears in any other MSBuild or NuGet config file.

         WHY THE FIX IS UNAVAILABLE IN v1.x: Aspire 13.6.0 and 13.6.5 require YamlDotNet 18.1.0+,
         and YamlDotNet is held at 16.x for the whole v1.x engine series because YamlDotNet types
         are part of the frozen Vouchfx.Sdk v1 surface (IStepBinder&lt;T&gt;.Bind takes a
         YamlNode). Raising it would make the CLI refuse external providers built against
         YamlDotNet 16.

         WHY THE PATH IS JUDGED UNREACHABLE (an inference from the code, not a tested result):
         the only production construction site, HeadlessTopology.cs, sets DisableDashboard =
         true. That flag can only be set in code (no configuration key or environment variable
         maps to it) and the host passes empty args, so the dashboard the advisory concerns is
         not started. No dashboard is shipped: Vouchfx.Cli.csproj sets
         SkipAddAspireDefaultReferences=true and Vouchfx.Dcp.targets sets a placeholder
         dashboard path instead of shipping the dashboard. vouchfx adds no YARP resource of its
         own. A suite can declare a YARP image under services:, but vouchfx still starts no
         dashboard alongside it. The published tool does bundle the Aspire.Hosting and
         Aspire.Hosting.AppHost 13.4.2 DLLs, so scanners reading its deps.json will report the
         advisory. A source check cannot prove unreachability. The guard in
         tests/Vouchfx.Engine.Compilation.Tests/AspireAuditSuppressionGuardTests.cs fails CI
         if any other audit suppression or audit-level override appears in an MSBuild or
         NuGet config file, if this entry is still present once the Aspire pin is 13.6.5 or
         later, if a production Aspire host is constructed outside HeadlessTopology.cs or the
         dashboard is not disabled, if a package id containing Yarp is referenced (including
         Aspire.Hosting.Yarp), or if AddYarp is called in engine source.

         Approved by the maintainer on 2026-11-11.

         REMOVAL CONDITION: delete this item when Aspire is pinned at 13.6.5 or later, which is
         when the YamlDotNet hold is lifted, expected with the v2 engine. The guard test fails
         once the pin reaches 13.6.5 while this item is still present. A second suppression
         needs a deliberate change to that guard. See also SECURITY.md. -->
    <NuGetAuditSuppress Include="https://github.com/advisories/GHSA-f7vh-h5g3-7jrh" />
  </ItemGroup>
</Project>
